From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:08:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 111/115] x86_64: SLP vectoriser for pairs of double
 assignments (SSE2)

optslp (optimizer switch SLP, on at -O4) turns two consecutive assignments
to adjacent doubles in memory (m2 at m1+8 through the same pointer or array
element) with right-hand sides of the same shape into one assignment of a
two-element double vector: adjacent loads become a vector load, equal
operands a broadcast, other operand pairs a pack; packs and broadcasts of
variables are kept in vector temps for later statements of the list, and two
local double assignments that later statements use as a pair become a vector
with its elements extracted. The second statement must not read memory that
may be the first one's target: accesses are compared by base and field
offset, and pointers whose every assignment is @a[i], a copy or inc/dec of
such a pointer point at whole elements of a (provenance), so different fields
do not overlap. With unmasked floating-point exceptions, an exception of the
second element is raised before the first result is stored (hence a switch).

New: in_vec2d_* intrinsics (pack, dup, add, sub, mul, div, lo, hi) with x86
code generation, drnf_unaligned derefs (element alignment, the reference of
the addressed operand without lea), vector temps in xmm registers, OS_M128
moves in the floating-point domain (movups instead of movdqu), unpck*pd and
packed operations in the MovapX chain rename.
nbody: the inner loop's dx/dy and the velocity updates, the position update;
tests/test/opt/tslp1.pp.
